 front of a camera) is gone; the problem of getting dirt on the film swept away.", "reference": "2023 March 16, John Boorman, “Today’s ‘films’ are nothing of the sort – so stop calling them that”, in The Guardian, →ISSN:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(flow cytometry) A line that separates particle type-clusters on two-dimensional dot plots.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A tally mark consisting of four vertical bars crossed by a diagonal, representing a count of five.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "An individual theme park as part of a larger resort complex with multiple parks.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "It would encompass more than 500 acres and include a new theme park, several hotels, two mammoth parking garages with direct access from the freeway and a \"third gate\" — land set aside for future expansion.", "reference": "1993 05, Rich Mannino, “The World According to Disney”, in Orange Coast Magazine, page 83:"}, {"text": "Disneyland opened its second gate – Disney's California Adventure. It was located exactly where Westcot would have been, directly across a central plaza from the Disneyland main gate.", "reference": "2006 August 1, Shaun Finnie, The Disneylands That Never Were, Lulu.com, →ISBN, page 168:"}, {"text": "At Disneyland Paris, the much-delayed “second gate,” a Walt Disney Studios theme park, opened on March 16.", "reference": "2008 December 9, James B. Stewart, Disneywar: The Battle for the Magic Kingdom, Simon and Schuster, →ISBN:"}, {"text": "For its part, Universal is also continuing to grow domestically, with its new second gate in Orlando – Volcano Bay – opening around the same time as Pandora.", "reference": "2018 April 16, John Reynolds, Cambridge IGCSE First Language English 4th edition, Hachette UK, →ISBN:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) A place where drugs are illegally sold.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The gangs were fighting for control of \"drug gates,\" control points for the sale of crack cocaine, heroin and marijuana.", "reference": "1996 April 24, “Connecticut: Task Force Successful In Curbing Street Gangs”, in Organized Crime Digest, volume 17, number 9, Annandale, V.A.: Washington Crime News Service, →OCLC, page 2, column 2:"}, {"text": "I put more guns in East Coast niggas' hands than East Coast niggas did when they came out here. I put them niggas on to more weed gates and weed spots and safe havens and safe spots than the East Coast did.", "reference": "1996 June, Tupac Shakur, quotee, “Inside the Mind of Shakur”, in Tupac Shakur, New York, N.Y.: Three Rivers Press, published 1998, →ISBN, page 101:"}, {"text": "The spatial mapping of Jamaica onto U.S. cities entails the erection of dance halls, reggae clubs, smoking yards or \"weed gates,\" select storefront vendors of Rasta apparel, ritual paraphernalia, and ital (\"natural\" and approved) foods (Hepner 1998: 206).", "reference": "2007, Paul Christopher Johnson, Diaspora Conversions: Black Carib Religion and the Recovery of Africa, Berkeley, C.A. […]: University of California Press, →ISBN, page 56:"}, {"text": "The very first dread I worked with, Dusty, had his gate at 55 Bowen.", "reference": "2018, U-God [Lamont Hawkins], Raw: My Journey Into The Wu-Tang, New York, N.Y.: Picador, →ISBN, →OCLC, page 69:"}, {"text": "There's not a gate in the West Side area that lasts longer than a month other than the ones I service. Gates go up and come down just as fast.", "reference": "2018, Robert Ricks, Fast Furious & Fatherless: An Urban Tale, [Morrisville, N.C.]: Lulu Publishing Services, →ISBN, page 244:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(dated, jive talk) A man; a male person.", "tags": ["dated"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Whatcha gonna say there, gate?", "reference": "1939, Cab Calloway, Frank Froeba, Jack Palmer, “Jumpin' Jive”:"}, {"text": "He said, \"Come on, gates, and jump with me / At the June Teenth Jamboree.\"", "reference": "1940, Louis Jordan, “June Teenth Jamboree”, performed by Louis Jordan and His Tympany Five:"}, {"text": "Louie wants you to get in there and lay yo' racket on that writin' machine so all the fine dinners and gates up in the land o' darkness will be hep and truck on down to this frolic pad 'cause the joint's really gonna be jumpin' and everythin' will be fine as wine like watermelon on the vine.", "reference": "1944 December 8, “Broadway Jam Session”, in The Tampa Times:"}], "synonyms": ["cat", "dude", "guy", "bloke", "boy", "bro", "broski", "bruh", "cat", "chap", "chappy", "chield", "cove", "covey", "dog [⇒ thesaurus] (slang)", "dawg", "dude", "fella", "fellow", "gadgie", "gate", "geezer", "gink", "gloak", "gome", "guy", "health", "homeboy", "homey", "joe", "lede", "nigga", "ninja", "omi", "pillicock", "punk", "rooster", "seg", "shalk", "wallah", "wer", "were", "wye"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(mining) A tunnel serving the coal face.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡeɪ̯t/", "tags": ["General American", "Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡæɪ̯t/", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈɡæ̝ɪ̯t]", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɡet/", "tags": ["Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɡeːt/", "tags": ["Wales"]}], "forms": [{"word": "gates", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "gating", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "gated", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "gated", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "yate", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To keep something inside by means of a closed gate.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, Oxbridge slang, dated or historical) To punish (a student) by not allowing to leave the college grounds.", "tags": ["dated", "historical", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "You climbed the wall, for which you ought to be gated; and finally, you came in blotto, for which you ought to be sent down.", "reference": "1935, Dorothy L. Sayers, Gaudy Night:"}, {"text": "“I’ve missed two lectures already,” remarked Maurice, who was breakfasting in his pyjamas.\n“Cut them all — he’ll only gate you.”", "reference": "1971, E. M. Forster, chapter 13, in Maurice, Penguin, published 1972, page 72:"}, {"text": "Dons could ring the front bell and be admitted after that hour. But students who returned after midnight or who stayed out all night were fined heavily or “gated” – that is, forbidden to leave college for several days.", "reference": "2010, Thomas J. Schaeper, Kathleen Schaeper, “Yanks and Brits”, in Rhodes Scholars, Oxford, and the Creation of an American Elite, New York, NY: Berghahn Books, →ISBN, page 52:"}], "synonyms": ["ground"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, biochemistry) To open (a closed ion channel).", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To furnish with a gate.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To turn (an image intensifier) on and off selectively, as needed or to avoid damage from excessive light exposure. See autogating.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To selectively regulate or restrict (access to something).", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Lillian walked the halls wearing a shirt plastered with what she assured everyone was a memetic stun agent; it looked just like the kill agent gating access to the SCP-001 database file, but as she patiently explained to McInnis, in art, context is everything.", "reference": "2024 September 28, HarryBlank, “Not Ready for Prime Time”, in SCP Foundation, archived from the original on 02 Oct 2024:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡeɪ̯t/", "tags": ["General American", "Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡæɪ̯t/", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈɡæ̝ɪ̯t]", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɡet/", "tags": ["Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɡeːt/", "tags": ["Wales"]}], "forms": [{"word": "gates", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(now Scotland, Northern England) A way, path.", "tags": ["Northern England", "Scotland"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I was going to be an honest man; but the devil has this very day flung first a lawyer, and then a woman, in my gate.", "reference": "1818 July 25, Jedediah Cleishbotham [pseudonym; Walter Scott], Tales of My Landlord, Second Series, […] (The Heart of Mid-Lothian), volume (please specify |volume=I to IV), Edinburgh: […] [James Ballantyne and Co.] for Archibald Constable and Company, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "\"Stand out o' my gate, wife, for, d'ye see, I am rather in a haste, Jean Linton.\"", "reference": "1828, James Hogg, Mary Burnet:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) A journey.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[…] nought regarding, they kept on their gate, / And all her vaine allurements did forsake […]", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book II, Canto XII”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(Scotland, Northern England) A street; now used especially as a combining form to make the name of a street e.g. \"Briggate\" (a common street name in the north of England meaning \"Bridge Street\") or Kirkgate meaning \"Church Street\".", "tags": ["Northern England", "Scotland"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(British, Scotland, dialect, archaic) Manner; gait.", "tags": ["British", "Scotland", "archaic", "dialectal"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gate", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}